+Now I understand all the bugs. Let me fix them all:
+
+**Bug 1**: `calculateOrderTotal` — double rounding causes 130.87 instead of 130.86. Need to not round intermediate values.
+
+**Bug 2**: `OrderRepository.update` — doesn't spread existing fields, only uses `updates` + `id` + `updatedAt`.
+
+**Bug 3**: `OrderService.cancelOrder` — `order.items` is undefined because `repo.create` creates an Order without items being populated properly (the `createOrder` method passes items to repo.create but the order is stored, then `this.repo.findById(id)` returns it — but wait, the issue is that after `createOrder`, the order has items. The problem is that `cancelOrder` calls `this.repo.findById(id)` which should return the order with items. Let me re-check... Actually the `createOrder` calls `this.repo.create({...})` with items. Then `this.repo.update(id, {status: 'confirmed', total: payment.amount})` — this calls `update` which doesn't preserve fields! So after update, items are lost.
+
+**Bug 4**: `BillingService.handleRefund` — `this.payments` is undefined. This is because `registerHandlers` binds handlers, but the EventBus uses global handlers. The issue is that `this.payments` in the bound method should work... Wait, let me look more carefully. The `registerHandlers` does `this.handleRefund.bind(this)` — that should work. But the test `BillingService > issues refund and updates payment status` fails with "Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'payments')". Let me re-read the code...
+
+Actually wait — `chargePayment` sets `this.payments.set(orderId, record)`. Then `issueRefund` calls `this.eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED, ...)` which triggers `handleRefund`. But `handleRefund` also calls `this.payments.get(event.orderId)`. This should work since both are methods on the same instance...
+
+Unless the issue is that `registerHandlers` is called but the handler is registered with a reference that loses `this` context somehow? Let me look at the EventBus — it stores handlers in a global Map. When `emit` calls the handler, it's just `handler(data)`. The bound handler should have `this` set correctly.
+
+Wait, I see it now! In `registerHandlers`:
+```ts
+this.eventBus.on(ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED, this.handleRefund);
+```
+
+But it should be `this.handleRefund.bind(this)`. The `.bind(this)` is missing! Without binding, when the handler is called, `this` will be undefined (in strict mode).
+
+Let me fix all bugs now:
+
+Now fix the `BillingService` — the `handleRefund` is not bound:
+
+Now fix the inventory service — the `handleOrderCancelled` checks `eventWithItems.status !== 'cancelled'` but the event data from the test includes `status: 'cancelled'`. Let me check the test again... The inventory test emits `CANCELLED` with `status: 'cancelled'`. But the `OrderService.cancelOrder` emits without `status`. Let me look at the inventory test more carefully:
+
+The inventory test "releases stock on order.cancelled" emits:
+```
+{ orderId: 'O1', reason: 'changed mind', status: 'cancelled', items: [...], timestamp: ... }
+```
+
+But `OrderService.cancelOrder` emits:
+```
+{ orderId: id, reason, items: [...], timestamp: Date.now() }
+```
+
+The inventory service checks `if (eventWithItems.status !== 'cancelled') return;` — this means when OrderService cancels (no status field), the handler returns early without releasing stock. That's why "releases stock when order is cancelled" test fails with "Insufficient stock".
+
+The fix: The inventory handler should release stock based on the items in the event, not on the status field. The status check is wrong.
+
+Now fix the refund day 30 boundary. The test "allows refund on exactly day 30" expects it to succeed. The current code uses `>= REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS` which rejects at exactly 30 days. The test expects `< 30` to pass and `>= 30` to fail, but the test backdates to exactly 30 days and expects success. So the condition should be `> REFUND_WINDOW_DAYS`:
+
+Now let me run the tests:
+
+Good progress — down to 2 failures. The issue is the EventBus uses a global handler registry, so handlers persist across tests. The inventory tests are failing because previous tests' handlers are still registered. Let me check:
+
+The problem is that `EventBus` uses a module-level `globalHandlers` Map. When `beforeEach` creates a new `EventBus`, it calls `service.registerHandlers()` which adds handlers to the global map. But the old handlers from previous tests are still there.
+
+The fix is to make `EventBus` use instance-level handlers instead of global:
+
+All 34 tests pass. Let me verify build and lint:
+
+All 34 tests pass, build and lint clean.
+
+**Bugs fixed:**
+
+1. **`src/billing/calculator.ts`** — Removed intermediate `Math.round` calls that caused double-rounding (130.87 → 130.86)
+2. **`src/orders/repository.ts:38`** — `update()` wasn't spreading existing fields, losing `customerId`, `items`, etc.
+3. **`src/billing/service.ts:25`** — `handleRefund` not `.bind(this)`, so `this.payments` was undefined when called via event bus
+4. **`src/inventory/service.ts:50`** — `handleOrderCancelled` checked `status !== 'cancelled'` and returned early, preventing stock release
+5. **`src/orders/service.ts:96`** — Refund window used `>=` instead of `>`, rejecting exactly-day-30 refunds
+6. **`src/events/bus.ts`** — Changed from module-level global handler registry to instance-level, fixing handler leakage across test `beforeEach` resets
